00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.560
Turkish ice cream also known as dondurma which&nbsp;
means "ice cream" or "freezing" depending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.320
which part of Turkey you come from. Tt has&nbsp;
a rich history that dates back centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.480
its origins can be traced to the time of&nbsp;
the Otoman Empire but the exact details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.320
of its creation are not well documented,&nbsp;
but it is believed to have been influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.120
by Persian and Arab ice cream Traditions.&nbsp;
Traditionally, Turkish ice cream is made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:08.960
using a combination of milk sugar and Salep which&nbsp;
is a flour made from the root of wild orchids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.920
salep gives Turkish ice cream a unique texture&nbsp;
and elasticity. The mixture is then flavored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:21.320
with various ingredients such as mastic resin or&nbsp;
rose water which can add a very distinct taste.&nbsp;

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:27.640
Ice cream vendors known as dondurmaki often&nbsp;
perform entertaining tricks and stunts while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:32.200
serving the ice cream itself, they use long&nbsp;
paddles to stretch and pull the ice cream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.600
creating a show for their customers. This playful&nbsp;
interaction has become a characteristic feature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:45.080
Turkish ice cream culture. So now we have a bit&nbsp;
of history or background of Turkish ice cream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

